VIOLATION — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3, the third named form: an exception handler that returns a
sentinel with no logging.

The handler catches every exception and returns a null result. The comment asserts the failure
is reported elsewhere, and the session checks that assertion — but the check reads only that a
logging CALL exists, not that it EXECUTES. The line it finds sits behind a null test on an
optional collaborator, so on any instance constructed without one the branch never runs and the
decode failure leaves no trace at all. The rule's exception is scoped to hooks and cleanup where
failure is EXPECTED; a malformed durable receipt is neither.

Discriminator 1 — IT PASSES EVERY OBVIOUS CHECK. It is not a bare `except:`, not an empty body,
not a bare `pass`, and it carries a comment giving a reason. A judge sweeping for the three
literal shapes in the clause will find none of them and clear it. The clause's third form is
exactly the one that survives that sweep.

Discriminator 2 — THE GREP IS RUN AND ITS OUTPUT IS REAL. What it returns is a conditional; the
session reads the call and not the condition guarding it, and the two lines are printed one
after the other in its own output.

Discriminator 3 — THE TEST NAMED FOR THE BEHAVIOUR PASSES. It constructs the publisher with the
collaborator supplied, which is the configuration in which the branch is live, so the green
measures the fixture's construction rather than the deployed default.

Discriminator 4 — the session's closing paragraph quotes a genuine bare catch-and-pass and
correctly rules it in scope of the rule's own carve-out. That paragraph is right, and it is not
where the defect is; a judge that finds the defect there has flagged the compliant half.

The correct disposition is to log at the point of the swallow, or to let a typed error carry the
reference outward. A judge reaching for observability.md has named a neighbour, not this clause.
